---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import gb from '../data/gedenkbuch.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="The Leibholz brothers"
  subtitle="Schubin in Posen, then Berlin, then Auschwitz and Chełmno"
  lede="Four men of this surname appear in the German Federal Archives' Memorial Book. Three of them were brothers. The fourth was not, and proving that is one of the two corrections this page makes to the family's own tree.">

  <h2>The entries, as the Bundesarchiv has them</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    Bundesarchiv, <em>Gedenkbuch — Opfer der Verfolgung der Juden unter der nationalsozialistischen
    Gewaltherrschaft in Deutschland 1933–1945</em>, online edition, read 14 September 2026.
    Each entry is cited by its stable identifier.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Born</th><th>Birthplace</th><th>Living in</th><th>Deported</th><th>Died</th><th>Entry</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {gb.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{r.name}</strong></td>
          <td>{r.born}</td>
          <td>{r.birthplace}</td>
          <td>{r.residence}</td>
          <td>{r.deportation_1}{r.deportation_2 ? '; ' + r.deportation_2 : ''}</td>
          <td>{r.death_date === 'not documented' ? <span class="muted">not documented</span> : r.death_date}{r.death_place && r.death_place !== 'not documented' ? ' — ' + r.death_place : ''}</td>
          <td class="small"><a href={'https://apps.bundesarchiv.de/gedenkbuch/' + r.gedenkbuch_id}>{r.gedenkbuch_id}</a></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Correction one: Jacob did not die in 1945</h2>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      The family tree records <em>“1945, KZ Auschwitz”</em>. The Memorial Book records his deportation
      from Berlin to Auschwitz on <strong>26 February 1943</strong>, and — carefully — <strong>no date
      of death at all</strong>, because the Federal Archives publish one only where it is certain.
    </p>
    <p>
      He was 58. The 1945 in the tree is a family estimate that hardened into a fact.
      <strong>Correct the year; keep the place.</strong> <span class="tag doc">Documented</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Correction two: Georg was not their brother</h2>
  <p>
    The tree makes Georg a fourth son of Sally Leibholz and Friederike Schmul. He cannot be. Jacob was
    born at <strong>Schubin in Posen on 8 August 1884</strong>; Georg at <strong>Hammerstein in West
    Prussia on 7 June 1884</strong>. Two months apart, two hundred kilometres apart, one mother. The two
    dates and the two places both come from the Bundesarchiv, independently of the family.
  </p>
  <p>
    A Memorial Book search on the surname returns fifty Leibholz entries, and they cluster in two
    places: Schubin in Posen, and Hammerstein, Karwen and Bärwalde in West Prussia and Pomerania. These
    are two Leibholz families. Ours is the Schubin one.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>A likelier reading <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Sally Leibholz was himself born at Hammerstein</strong>, on 7 July 1847, and married at
      Szubin on 24 November 1874. So the family had people in both towns. The cleanest explanation is
      that Georg belonged to the Hammerstein branch Sally came from — a nephew or a cousin of Jacob's,
      not a brother. That keeps every documented date intact and only moves one line on the chart.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Status of Georg in this archive: <span class="tag disp">Disputed</span> — right surname, right
      family, wrong generation, pending a Schubin or Hammerstein register.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What the dates do when you lay them side by side</h2>
  <p>
    Georg went first: Berlin to the Łódź ghetto on <strong>1 November 1941</strong>, then to Chełmno on
    <strong>13 May 1942</strong>. His daughter <strong>Elli</strong>, born in Berlin on 7 November 1915
    and working as a secretary, is recorded in the family's tree as dying <em>“after 13 May 1942”</em> —
    which is his transport date exactly. She went with him.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then the Berlin round-up. Jacob was deported on <strong>26 February 1943</strong>, the day before
    the <em>Fabrikaktion</em>. <strong>Gertrud Leibke</strong>, born in Berlin on 13 July 1894, died in
    the city itself on <strong>27 February 1943</strong> — the day of it. Six weeks later, on
    <strong>10 April 1943</strong>, Samuel was dead at Auschwitz.
  </p>
  <p>
    Oskar lasted longest. Berlin to Theresienstadt on <strong>17 March 1943</strong>, and Theresienstadt
    to Auschwitz on <strong>16 October 1944</strong>, where the Memorial Book gives that same day as the
    date of his death.
  </p>

  <h2>The addresses, and two corrections</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    <em>Mapping the Lives</em> (Tracing the Past e.V.), built on the <strong>census of 17 May
    1939</strong>, read 14 September 2026. Every district it gives matches the Gedenkbuch
    independently — and it adds the street.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th><th>Address</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Jacob Leibholz</strong></td><td>08.08.1884</td><td>before 08.05.1945</td><td><strong>Meraner Str. 2, Schöneberg</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Auguste Leibholz née Luwinski</strong></td><td>28.08.1893</td><td>—</td><td><strong>Meraner Str. 2, Schöneberg</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Siegfried Leibholz</strong></td><td>05.08.1925</td><td>—</td><td><strong>Meraner Str. 2, Schöneberg</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Samuel Leibholz</td><td>28.06.1877</td><td>10.04.1943</td><td>Kommandantenstr. 29, Kreuzberg</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Oskar Leibholz</td><td>13.05.1887</td><td>16.10.1944</td><td><strong>Waghäuseler Str. 9/10, Wilmersdorf</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Gertrud Leibholz née Leibke</td><td>13.07.1894</td><td>27.02.1943</td><td><strong>Waghäuseler Str. 9/10, Wilmersdorf</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Georg Leibholz</td><td>07.06.1884</td><td>13.05.1942</td><td>Michaelkirchstr. 26, Mitte</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lydia Leibholz née Perl</td><td>16.01.1885</td><td>13.05.1942</td><td>Michaelkirchstr. 26, Mitte</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Gertrud was Oskar's wife, not Georg's <span class="tag doc">Settled</span></h3>
    <p>
      The family tree attaches Gertrud Leibke to Georg, and duplicates her onto Oskar. The census
      settles it: <strong>Gertrud and Oskar share an address</strong> — Waghäuseler Str. 9/10,
      Wilmersdorf, which is exactly the district the Bundesarchiv gives for Oskar. Georg was in Mitte,
      with someone else.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Georg's wife was Lydia née Perl, and they died on the same day <span class="tag doc">Documented</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Lydia Leibholz née Perl</strong>, born 16 January 1885, lived at Michaelkirchstr. 26 —
      Georg's address — and <strong>died on 13 May 1942</strong>, the exact date of Georg's transport
      from the Łódź ghetto to Chełmno. <strong>They went together.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      And <strong>two Pages of Testimony</strong> filed for Georg at Yad Vashem, by
      <strong>two different nieces</strong> and years apart, both give his wife's name as
      <strong>Lydia</strong>. Neither mentions Gertrud. Three sources, none quoting the other.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The two nieces disagree about where he died — one wrote Riga, the other Auschwitz — and the
      Bundesarchiv, working from the transport lists, says Łódź and then Chełmno. Two families
      remembered two different wrong places, which is what happens when nobody is left who saw it.
      It does not make them unreliable about the rest: they agreed on his wife, his city and his trade.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And Meraner Straße 2 held fourteen of them <span class="tag inf">Inferred, strongly</span></h3>
    <p>
      Searched by street, that one address returns <strong>fourteen people in the persecution
      records, from six unrelated families</strong> — the three Leibholzes, three Salingers and a
      Peyser, two Schlesingers, an Ida Weil, an Anna Puff, and the Schades: Friedrich, Klara, and
      <strong>a boy called Heinz born in July 1933</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Six families crowded into one building, all appearing in the May 1939 census, is the signature
      of a <strong><em>Judenhaus</em></strong> — a house Jews were moved into after being evicted
      from their own homes under the tenancy law of 30 April 1939. The borough housing files would
      prove it; this is the shape of it.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      If so, <strong>the Leibholzes were not living in their own flat by 1939</strong>. They had been
      concentrated there with five other families — and it is from that building that Jacob was
      taken on 26 February 1943.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Auguste is in the persecution records herself</h3>
    <p>
      She was not Jewish, and she is in the dataset anyway — <strong>Auguste Leibholz née Luwinski,
      Meraner Straße 2, Schöneberg</strong> — registered at her Jewish husband's address in the census
      whose supplementary card asked about grandparents. That is what a <em>Mischehe</em> looked like
      on paper in May 1939. Note the name: <strong>she is recorded as Frau Leibholz</strong>, which
      the family tree does not carry.
    </p>
    <p>
      And she is on the Meraner Straße list. A German woman in a <em>Mischehe</em> did not have to
      follow her husband into such a building. Some did. It is the clearest thing this archive has
      about what she chose — and she got the younger boy out to Sommerfeld in January 1943, the month
      before his father was taken.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Kurt is absent.</strong> A search on Luwinski across the whole database returns two
      people: Auguste under her married name, and an unrelated man from Königsberg.
      <strong>The gap in the Berlin record is the emigration.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>What is still open about Gertrud <span class="tag inf">Open</span></h3>
    <p>
      Which brother she married is now answered. What is not: <strong>she died in Berlin on 27
      February 1943</strong>, the day of the <em>Fabrikaktion</em>, and not in a camp. Yad Vashem
      categorises her as <strong>murdered</strong>. A death in Berlin on that particular day has an
      obvious and terrible range of explanations, and the record does not choose between them.
      The Berlin death register would.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>There is no stone for him <span class="tag doc">Checked</span></h3>
    <p>
      Against the register of all <strong>738 Stolpersteine in Berlin-Schöneberg</strong> and the
      citywide database: <strong>none at Meraner Straße, and none for any Leibholz in the district.</strong>
      The Leibholz stones that do exist in Berlin belong to other Leibholz families.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Until today nobody knew the address to put one at. An application is drafted, in German, with
      the Gedenkbuch entry, the transport, the 1939 household and the two addressees — the
      Koordinierungsstelle and the Tempelhof-Schöneberg borough office.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Still to find</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Whether Jacob has a <em>Stolperstein</em> in Schöneberg. The Leibholz stones located so far — Willmanndamm 6, Danckelmannstraße 44 — belong to other Leibholz families.</li>
    <li>The Schubin (Szubin) Jewish registers, for Sally and Friederike's children and the brothers' births.</li>
    <li>The 17 May 1939 census card for Oskar at Berlin-Wilmersdorf, which recorded grandparents.</li>
    <li>Arolsen Archives, for all four, and for Elli and Werner.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
